## Gates — report PASS / FAIL / BLOCKED for each, with evidence

**G1 — A1 (empty canvas).** The early return that made an empty plan ignore 
clicks is gone; the
default view box is real and finite; nothing else depended on the early return 
(check the whole
function, and that the tool branches are reached). The test that claims to 
prove it must actually
start from a new model and drive clicks.

**G2 — A2 (levels from the document).** In the plan path there must be no 
hard-coded level id
(`ElementId(10)` / `ElementId(11)`) or hard-coded level name deciding anything:
the plan tabs,
`plan_level`, the plan export, the Project Browser's plan entries and the 
Properties "View Name".
A const fallback name is acceptable; a const deciding the *level* is not. A 
project with no level
must refuse a drawing tool. Look for remaining hard-coded ids anywhere in 
`apps/desktop/src` and
say which are in the plan path and which are not.

**G3 — A3 (two-stage Esc).** Through the real window path (`global_keys` then 
the viewport), the
first Esc must step back inside a tool that has work pending and the second 
must leave it. Check
`tool_has_pending_points` against every tool that keeps pending state in the 
viewport (a tool whose
pending state is missing from that list leaves the tool on the first Esc — find
any).

**G4 — A4 (keys and keymap).** Ctrl+S, Ctrl+N, Ctrl+O and the typed shortcuts 
reach commands; typing
in a text field is never stolen; the Save As button exists, is wired and has an
icon. Check the
matcher replay in `keymap_key` for correctness: a dead-end sequence must clear,
a half-typed
sequence must do nothing, and a matched command must run once, not twice.

**G5 — A5 (options bar per tool).** No command inherits another's numbers. 
`command_defaults` must
cover every field the command arms used to set inline — diff the arms against 
the table and report
any value that was silently dropped (a command that no longer sets what it used
to). Check the duct
and pipe paths still see their sizes before `choose_tool` runs.

**G6 — A6 (level authoring).** `place_level` refuses a duplicate name (any 
case) and a level closer
than `MIN_LEVEL_GAP_M`; `rename_level` and `delete_level` behave as documented 
(delete refuses a
level with elements on it, rename refuses a name in use); `Command::Rename` now
accepts a level —
check this cannot rename something it should not, and that no test asserted the
old refusal. The
options-bar UI must call them and report the outcome.

**G7 — A7 (ribbon honesty).** `IMPLEMENTED` must agree exactly with the arms of
`run_command`:
extract both lists yourself and compare (report any id in one and not the 
other, in either
direction). The gate must prevent an unwired command from running. A disabled 
button must not be
clickable. The honesty test's exclusions (the file-dialog commands) must be 
justified and its
coverage stated. The `debug_assert` must actually be reachable for a listed 
command with no arm.

**G8 — A8 (whole window + end-to-end).** `Desktop::window` must be the same 
code the app ran before
(diff the moved body against `c90cbc8`; report any behavioural difference, 
including panel order and
the global-key/clipboard order). The `ribbon_hits` field must not exist in a 
production build and
must not affect rendering. The office test must exercise the journey it claims 
(new project, three
levels, a grid, four walls, a floor, a room, a section, save, reopen, IFC 
export) and its
assertions must be able to fail (name the assertion that would catch a 
regression in each step).

**G9 — the fix branch.** `048036b` must be test-only, must clear both texture 
deltas, and must not
change product code. Confirm the failure it fixes is a `debug_assert!` in 
epaint and therefore
invisible to `cargo test --release` (which is what CI runs).

